                            {
                                "ReferenceNumber": 1,
                                "Value": {
                                    "StringWithMarkup": [
                                        {
                                            "String": "General Background\n\nGinsenosides or panaxosides are a class of steroid glycosides and triterpene saponins found exclusively in the plant genus  TAX-4053. They are the principal components responsible for the pharmaceutical activities of ginseng.\n\nGinsenosides are composed of an aglycone and sugar moieties. Based on the structure of their aglycones, ginsenosides have been categorized into three broad types; Protopanaxadiol-type-ginsenosides (PPD), which contain hydroxyl groups at positions 3 and 20, Protopanaxatriol-type-ginsenosides (PPT), which contain hydroxyl groups at positions 3, 6 and 20, and Oleanonic-acid-type-ginsenosides.\n\nAlthough ginsenosides only have three kinds of aglycones, more than 60 kinds of ginsenosides have been identified by now due to the variability in the type of sugar moieties, their number, and the carbon positions they link to.\n\nThe biological activity of ginsenoside increases with the decrease of the number of the sugar moieties. All of the ginsenosides found in large quantities in wild ginseng (known as major ginsenosides) are heavily glycosylated, while the more active less-glycosylated ginsenosides are found in much smalller quantities (and are commonly referred to as the\nminor ginsenosides). Following oral intake of ginseng, the large low-solubility and poor-permeability major ginsenosides cannot be absorbed by the human body directly [Su06], and are deglycosylated into smaller forms in the gastrointestinal tract by intestinal bacteria and/or digestive enzymes [8923812][11748374]|.\n\nGlycosidase enzymes that can convert the major ginsenosides to minor ginsenosides are not common and are of commercial interest.\n\nThe compounds CPD-9249, CPD-12102 and CPD-15444 have been shown to have anticancer activity. CPD-12102 inhibited tumor angiogenesis in in vitro studies [PMID: 18781130] and has inhibitory effect on breast cancer in mice [PMID: 18999937]. Studies have shown that ovarian cancer metastasis is inhibited by CPD-12102 [PMID: 18959116]. CPD-15451 has inhibitory effects on tumor angiogenesis in mice [7522731][8845804]. CPD-12102 was recently produced as an anti-angiogenic and anti-cancer drug in China. \n\nAbout This Pathway\n\nLike other saponines, ginsenosides provide protection to the plants from fungal pathogens like trichoderma [PMID: 12946424]. However certain fungal pathogens like TAX-36331 and TAX-29920 thrive on the ginsenosides in the roots. It appears that Panax plants and soil borne microbe have allelopathic interactions [PMID: 16242739]. This above pathway shows the sequential deglycosylation of CPD-9250 to CPD-12100 by two ginsenosidases characterized in the fungal pathogen TAX-36331 [PMID: 19818460].",
                                            "Markup": [
